  <header class="top">
    <p class="eyebrow">For DSP teams &amp; hardware makers</p>
    <h1>License the engine, not the plugin.</h1>
    <p class="standfirst"><code>harmonic_core</code> is the additive-synthesis
      oscillator behind Cosine, sold separately from the plugin: a
      <b>zero-dependency, no_std Rust crate with a plain C ABI</b> you can
      drop into a hardware synth, a plugin of your own, a game, or an
      embedded product. No plugin UI, no tier gate, no dependency on a DAW
      host — just the DSP.</p>

    <div class="facts">
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">Dependencies</p><p class="v">Zero</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">Footprint</p><p class="v">~14 KB cdylib</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">Voice struct</p><p class="v">624 B, POD</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">Allocates / panics</p><p class="v">Never</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">Targets</p><p class="v">ARM · RISC-V · wasm32</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><p class="k">License</p><p class="v">AGPL-3.0 or commercial</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout teal">
      <span class="lbl">In one sentence</span>
      Every note is a closed-form sum of cosines — a spectral tilt plus one
      resonant hump — computed directly, with no wavetable and no
      per-partial loop, so the cost per sample stays flat whether you're
      rendering 3 harmonics or 1200. Full derivation:
      <a href="/monograph">the scientific monograph</a>.
    </div>
  </header>

