  <section id="what">
    <h2><span class="n">1</span>What you're licensing</h2>
    <p class="lead">A DSP core, not a product. You build the instrument
      around it.</p>
    <p><code>harmonic_core</code> owns exactly one job: turning a note
      (frequency, a spectral-tilt parameter, a few shaping controls) into
      audio samples. It doesn't open a window, doesn't talk to a DAW host,
      doesn't manage polyphony or mixing — that's your product's job, the
      same way it's <code>harmonic_synth</code>'s job in Cosine itself.
      What you get is the oscillator math, the character/filter/envelope
      stages built on top of it, and the exact same code Cosine ships in
      production — not a stripped-down or re-implemented version.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="lbl">Why not just write your own additive oscillator</span>
      The naive version — a bank of per-partial sine oscillators, or a
      wavetable rebuilt whenever the spectrum changes — either costs more
      CPU as the tone gets brighter, or aliases when you push it. The
      closed-form sum here costs the same at 3 harmonics or 1200
      (measured: 530&times; realtime per voice, under 3% spread), and the
      partial count is clamped to Nyquist by construction, so the clean
      oscillator can't alias. That's the whole reason to license this
      instead of building it from scratch.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="targets">
    <h2><span class="n">2</span>Where it runs</h2>
    <p class="lead">The same code, unmodified, across every target below —
      that's the point of a zero-dependency <code>no_std</code> crate.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Verified as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>x86-64 (desktop plugin)</td><td>Cosine itself: VST3 + CLAP, Windows</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cortex-M4F / M7</td><td>Daisy Seed (hardware-synth class MCU)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cortex-M0 (soft-float)</td><td>builds and passes the core test suite</td></tr>
          <tr><td>RISC-V</td><td>builds and passes the core test suite</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AArch64</td><td>bit-exact cross-check against x86-64 output</td></tr>
          <tr><td>wasm32</td><td>an 8-voice browser demo — <a href="/demo">try it live</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Cross-compiling clean to that range is only possible because the
      crate has <strong>no dependencies at all</strong> — no <code>libm</code>
      (every trig function is hand-rolled in <code>trig.rs</code>), no
      allocator, no threading, no floating-point library assumptions beyond
      IEEE 754. Full contract: <code>docs/08_EMBEDDED_INTEGRATION.md</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="guarantees">
    <h2><span class="n">3</span>The guarantees, and how they're checked</h2>
    <p class="lead">Every claim below is something a real-time audio thread
      needs, and every one is checked in CI on every push — not just
      asserted in a doc.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Never allocates, never panics</td><td>no global allocator, no <code>unwrap</code>/<code>expect</code>/<code>panic!</code> in non-test code; <code>panic = "abort"</code> in release</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Never produces NaN / &plusmn;&infin;</td><td>every public setter clamps out-of-range input by construction; an adversarial stress suite feeds NaN/&plusmn;&infin;/parameter storms into every setter</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bit-identical output</td><td>the same render, byte-for-byte, on x86-64 / ARM / wasm32, at 48 and 96 kHz, at any block size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Test coverage</td><td>120 core tests (11 of them the adversarial RT-safety suite), run under QEMU for ARM and Node for wasm32, on every push</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Independently measured</td><td>~26M samples/s per clean voice (&asymp;550&times; realtime @ 48 kHz), flat within 1% from 3 to 1200 harmonics</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A render being reproducible across machines matters for a hardware
      product the same way it matters for Cosine's own freeze/bounce
      guarantee: a shared <code>verify</code> module renders two scripted
      passes and every target checks its output against the same 64-bit
      hash. Full methodology: <code>docs/06_VERIFICATION.md</code>; the
      independent audit against the shipped plugin build:
      <a href="/#audit">on the main site</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="integrate">
    <h2><span class="n">4</span>Integrating it</h2>
    <p class="lead">A plain C ABI, one struct you own the memory for, no
      hidden state.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You own the memory.</strong> <code>Voice</code> is a
        repr(C), plain-old-data struct (624 bytes on 64-bit targets,
        no internal pointers) — the crate never allocates it for you.
        Call <code>harmonic_voice_size()</code> / <code>harmonic_voice_align()</code>
        at runtime rather than hardcoding the size; it has grown before
        (LFO, pan, FM, pitch bend) and will again.</li>
      <li><strong>Polyphony is your policy.</strong> The crate renders one
        voice at a time; how many voices you run, how you steal or mix
        them, is entirely up to your host — same as it is for
        <code>harmonic_synth</code> itself.</li>
      <li><strong>No I/O, no locks, no syscalls</strong> anywhere in the
        render path — safe to call from a hard-real-time interrupt handler.</li>
      <li><strong>~28 exported functions</strong> covering voice sizing,
        init, note on/off, every parameter setter, and per-sample
        rendering. Full signatures: <code>docs/05_API_REFERENCE.md</code>;
        architecture and data flow: <code>docs/03_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries">
    <h2><span class="n">5</span>What it won't do</h2>
    <p class="lead">Stated plainly, the same way Cosine's own limits are
      published rather than buried.</p>
    <div class="cando">
      <div class="yes">
        <h4>It's built for</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>A spectral tilt plus one resonant hump, computed live</li>
          <li>Additive tones that must stay CPU-flat as they brighten</li>
          <li>A product that needs the same render on more than one
            architecture</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="no">
        <h4>Not built for</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Arbitrary per-harmonic envelopes or several independent
            formants — that's a filter bank or a different engine</li>
          <li>A general O(1) additive synth for any spectrum shape</li>
          <li>UI, hosting, mixing, or polyphony management — that's your
            product, same as <code>harmonic_synth</code> is Cosine's</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Full list, with the reasoning behind each one:
      <code>docs/07_LIMITATIONS.md</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="licensing">
    <h2><span class="n">6</span>Licensing &amp; pricing</h2>
    <p class="lead">Open for open source and evaluation. Paid to ship
      closed.</p>
    <div class="callout teal">
      <span class="lbl">AGPL-3.0-only</span>
      Free to use, including commercially, as long as your product's
      source (or the network service built on it) is itself available
      under AGPL-3.0. Good for evaluation, research, and genuinely
      open-source products.
    </div>
    <div class="callout amber">
      <span class="lbl">Commercial license</span>
      Lifts the AGPL's copyleft and network-use obligations for a
      closed-source product — a hardware synth's firmware, a proprietary
      plugin, a game. Per-product, per-seat, or royalty; terms and
      pricing are worked out per deal. An integration + determinism
      sign-off package for your own CI is available as a fixed add-on.
    </div>
    <p>
      <a class="btn" href="mailto:cityobukhov@gmail.com?subject=harmonic_core%20commercial%20license">Tell us what you're building &rarr;</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="https://github.com/Gnomir/AudioSynth/blob/main/LICENSE-COMMERCIAL.md">Read LICENSE-COMMERCIAL.md</a>
    </p>
  </section>

